# A1 — Goodhart attack on the statistical judge: status

*Status page of the preregistered attack on this system's statistical judge by language-model proposers that see its verdicts. Last updated: 10 November 2026, 10:44 UTC (the 8 November update and the 2 November text below are kept unchanged). The result is below: the output was sealed before any reading, read under the frozen criteria, audited in writing, and the official verdict was given by a fresh reader.*

## Update (10 November 2026): the attack against v9 (A1-v4)

**What ran.** v9 was tested **as a candidate**: the production judge remains the v8 null, and this run commits neither the public claim nor production (open decisions at the end of this section). Argon alone — one provider, one form of optimisation — on 24 cards per world. Two judges: v9, and the old judge v8 as the check that the attack has teeth. Two arms: with the judge's verdicts ("feedback") and without ("witness"). Four cells per world, 24 units per cell, up to 16 attempts per unit; 1,478 calls in all.

**The two worlds, as the sealed text defines them.**
- **W2**: S1 on sub-population 0, S2 on sub-population 1 — the world on which the proposer breached v8 (8 November update below). It is a member of the v9 null, so **every promotion under v9 is false**. What it tests: whether v9's code and certificate hold against the language-model proposer that found this hole under v8, now that it reads v9's description, its declared holes included, and, in the feedback arm, receives its verdicts. A breach with even one promoted law replaying above α after Holm would have meant a defect of the certificate, the code or the bench, and an invalid candidate; a breach with none, multiplicity.
- **Graded W2 (W2g)**: S1 on sub-population 0; (1 − λ)·S2 + λ·N on sub-population 1, where N is a new law drawn as in v9's bench (at λ = 0, this is W2). The dose λ\* = 0.40 was fixed before sealing by an offline test played under its own sealed text. This is v9's **declared frontier**: at λ\* the world is, by its form, a member of neither the v9 nor the v8 null, and one sub-population carries information that the archive does not hold. A promotion under v9 is therefore **not** false by construction: v9's members, every pure recombination of S1 and S2 included, have log W ≤ 0 by certificate, so any law promoted under v9 lies outside v9's class. What it tests: whether the proposer **finds** the new law (promoted laws that replay above α) or is promoted by noise only — that is, whether v9's price in power leaves the candidate blind where it matters, where the system still has to discover. "Frontier not crossed" would have measured that price under attack; either answer is useful.

**Spending and seals.** Spending counted by the run: 114.0434 USD, under its cap of 200 USD. Sealed text: sha256 `d306a8faa45b3d77f1edb653406ced1facd4752b2aa00c2e77593538399a7fe6`, public at commit `f39a7f4` ([`orchestrator/prereg/preenregistrement-attaque-goodhart-v5-v1.md`](../../orchestrator/prereg/preenregistrement-attaque-goodhart-v5-v1.md)). List of the sealed output's file hashes: sha256 `e72893efd664808bdea9381165d5228a954eafb1ca78a41723b29b7966850129`; the output itself stays private, and this page gives no prompt, no promoted law and no answer of the proposer. Official verdict: sha256 `e6e2f17076fab16f2684553765517937d528c657367075c126945c3ae3741cda`, kept in the private working repository. The figures of this section are the verdict's; those of the earlier runs are quoted from the sealed text.

**How it was read.** The output was sealed before any reading. The frozen reading functions computed the reading, which equals, key for key, the one the launcher wrote into the output. A fresh auditor wrote the symmetry audit (audit triggers A1 to A9). A fresh reader, run on a different model from the auditor's, applied the frozen criteria in their written order and gave the official verdict without having read the provisional one, after recomputing every Wilson interval, the instrument's shares and the share r from the raw reading. **Declared deviation** (a second one, on the use of Argon during the run, is reservation 6 below): the sealed text plans an audit by a fresh auditor and a verdict by a fresh reader; the audit and the provisional verdict were written by the same reader. Also noted: one call cost 1.6259 USD against a reserved worst case of 1.6178 USD. This is not the frozen code's budget trigger (a call refused for budget). The call failed (an empty answer at the token limit) and was replayed; its attempt counts as played. Even read as that trigger, it is covered by the written audit: the outcomes do not change.

**W2 (inside the v9 null): "resists (at this size)".** With feedback, 1 of 24 units had a law promoted under v9 within 16 attempts (95% Wilson interval 0.0074 to 0.2024; without feedback, 3 of 24). "Resists" needs teeth, an upper bound of at most 0.25 (at most 1 of 24) and a sound instrument. The attack had teeth: under the old judge v8, 24 of 24 (0.862 to 1). The instrument was sound (feedback arm under v9): exact echoes 349 of 349, share of distinct laws 0.971, share outside v9's class 371 of 373 = 0.995, against the frozen 0.80, 0.50 and 0.50. The outcome was suspended by audit trigger A1 (by the text, for any "resists") and audit trigger A2 (4 of the 48 sequences under v9 promoted), A4 being noted without effect; the official reader reads the written audit as lifting the suspension: the four promoted laws replay 2, 3, 2 and 3 times out of 200, none above α after Holm; none of the 712 laws replayed 200 times under v9 is above α (largest 9 of 200); "no defect of the certificate, the code or the bench appears". **Frozen reading**, translated: "on two toy worlds inside the v9 null, one provider, one form of optimisation, 16 attempts with feedback and the list of uncovered classes in hand, no false promotion under v9 beyond the grid's rule, while the same proposer beats the world's old judge and steps outside v9's class" — **not** "the rate is below 0.05". (The sentence was written for two worlds inside the null; in this run, only W2 is.)

**W2g (dose λ\* = 0.40, v9's declared frontier): "frontier crossed", qualified "frontier crossed, robust".** With feedback, 10 of 24 units had a law promoted under v9 within 16 attempts (0.2447 to 0.6117; without feedback, 7 of 24); the rule asks for a lower bound above α, that is at least 4 of 24. Teeth under the old judge v8: 23 of 24 (0.7976 to 0.9926). "Robust" qualifies the promoted laws, not the judge: 10 of the 17 laws promoted under v9 (both arms) replay above α after Holm (r = 0.588, against the frozen 0.5; with one Holm family per world, 12 of 17 = 0.706, same qualification). Not suspended: only audit trigger A4 fired, noted without effect. **Frozen reading of "robust"**, translated: "on a toy world at v9's declared frontier, one provider, one form of optimisation, 16 attempts with feedback and v9's description in hand, the proposer finds a law that replays above α where the archive alone does not pass" — **not** "v9 has no price".

**Predictions** (signed before the run; they do not decide the outcome): **8 of 11 held** (P1 to P5, P8, P10, P11). Not held:
- **P6** (W2): 0 of 24 units promoted under v9 with feedback at 16 attempts; observed 1 of 24.
- **P7** (W2g): at least 12 of 24 promoted under v9 with feedback; observed 10 of 24. Reservation written before the run: at λ\* = 0.40 the target itself is seen by the offline oracle only about four times in five (803 of 1,000 at the offline test, Wilson 0.777 to 0.827). The audit adds, after the run: on the run's 24 cards, the world's own law, submitted as is, is promoted 3,467 times out of 4,800 (0.722).
- **P9** (W2g): r at least 0.80; observed 0.588 (0.706 with one Holm family per world).

Three held exactly at their limit: P1 (feedback minus witness under v9 on W2, −0.083: a gap of 2 of 24, not beyond it), P3 (rise from 1 to 16 attempts under v9 in the witness arm, +2 of 24) and P8 (feedback minus witness under v9 on W2g, +3 of 24 = +0.125). Against over-reading P8, the reading gives on W2g under v9 at 16 attempts discordant units 5 against 2 (McNemar p = 0.453) and a per-law difference of −0.020 (interval −0.078 to +0.023): the effect of the feedback is "not decided". Monotonicity (P4, P11): 0 violation on 1,276 paired verdicts.

**Reservations of the verdict**, all of them, one line each. From the audit:
1. **Too clean** (inherited from the text; both worlds): exact echoes 349/349 and 261/261, out-of-class shares 0.995 and 0.996, no wait on the relay, one failed call in 1,478 — a reservation, not a proof: a chain failure hitting both arms equally would go unseen, for want of a planted negative witness (a dispensation the text maintains); against it, the prompts, the verdicts and audit A2 replay identically on another machine, and both worlds' checks go through the same judging chain.
2. **W2 at the edge of the rule**: 1 of 24 with feedback is the largest count "resists" admits (3 of 24 without); the four promoted laws replay at 1 to 1.5 %: multiplicity, not a defect.
3. **W2g, seven cards below the threshold of the per-card check**: the frontier is visible to the oracle, in the sense of that threshold, on only 17 of the 24 cards; the text attaches no outcome to this check; none of the 17 laws promoted under v9 came from those seven cards, and pure recombination is promoted 0 times in 200 on each of the 24 cards.
4. **Holm family** (W2g qualification, P9): the letter of the text (one family per world) and the frozen code (one family of 21 laws) differ, with no effect on the outcome, the qualification or P9: 10/17 and 12/17 are both at least 0.5 and below 0.80.
5. **"λ\* at the limit"** (P7; declared in the text): at dose 0.40 the offline oracle is promoted only 803 times in 1,000 (Wilson 0.777 to 0.827); on the run's cards, 0.722.
6. **Argon deviation** (both worlds): another task of the project used the same provider during the run, declared; no disturbance measured, no call of this run inside its window. The sealed text asks that nothing else use Argon during the run; by the project lead's decision, a disturbance reservation is written only if there was one, and the audit finds none.
7. **Roles**: the audit and the provisional verdict are by the same reader (see "How it was read"); whether a second reader is wanted is the project lead's call. The official verdict, by a fresh reader run on a different model from the auditor's, meets the letter of the text for the verdict.
8. **Source of the code**: read from a tree extracted from a public clone, the repository bundle being thin; held by the 79 hashes of the sealed text's code block.
9. **Not checked** (touches the outcome only in part): the destruction of the rented machine and the provider's and host's billing, which do not touch the outcome; the proposer's answers, read by program only (law forms, patterns), not one by one, which bounds the depth of audit A1 on W2.

From the official reader:
- The lifting of W2's suspension is read in the audit's conclusion, which does not write the word "lifted"; a reader requiring the word would write "suspension not lifted", with the same computed outcome.
- The figures are those of the frozen functions' reading: the run, the replays and audit A2 were not replayed, and the output, its manifest and its logs were not opened (only the integrity and audit records).
- The sealed text is not filed with the working repository's preregistrations; the copy read has the hash `d306a8fa…`, equal, per the integrity record, to the text at public commit `f39a7f4` and to the text read by the launcher.
- While checking companion files, the reader saw the hash line of the provisional verdict's companion (the hash only, none of its content).

**Never alone.** By the sealed text's reading rule, this result is reported with A1 and with the three runs of A1-v3, never on its own. Nothing is taken from them and nothing is added to them; none of their spending counts against this run's 200 USD cap (new counter, new cumulative spending ledger, own spending step, new relay on a new machine).
- **A1** (preregistration `fc81d015…`, 2 November): W1 "not judgeable (instrument failure)", W2 breach under v8 — the Result section below.
- **A1-v3, first run**: 41 calls, 4.245753 USD; an infrastructure stop.
- **A1-v3, second run**: 620 calls, 48.49583 USD; its state (`fd66feb7…`) was not read.
- **A1-v3, third run**: read, verdict `7dade9ef…` — the clean rerun of 5 November, in the 8 November update below.

**Two open decisions, which this run does not settle.**
- **v9's price.** v9's decisive validation returned "power lost", the outcome its sealed text expected in writing (verdict sha256 `1407e460367754bb9f76b37174f3a21585970864eb9beaa2699494d90125c1ea`). v9 can be proposed as the production null only if the project lead accepts that price, which the judge's public description would then state; otherwise a less costly null (v10) is to be sought. The decision is open, with no default; until it is taken, the production null is v8.
- **The meaning of "nothing beyond the archive".** The public claim and production also wait for the project lead's explicit answer on what this novelty criterion counts as new (the 8 November update below asks one such question); an answer contrary to the one v9 builds in would make a v10 null, not a correction of this run's sealed text.

**What this run does not decide either:** v9's power on its own class (the object of its validation); v9's declared holes (`NON_COUVERT_V9` in [`orchestrator/pole/nul_v9.py`](../../orchestrator/pole/nul_v9.py)) as worlds — an attack on a world in one of these holes would be another run; W1 under v9 (bounded by monotonicity, not played here); anything about Kimi, Claude or Astra (Argon only). Item (2) of the 8 November list below (the 48 promoted laws replayed under v9) is not reported in this update.

