# Ideation organism — an automated research pole that cannot grade its own homework

**What it is.** A small, fully inspectable system in which language-model agents from several providers propose research hypotheses on AI control, and in which, following a principle already published (see Related work below), *none of the agents that produce an idea is allowed to decide whether it survives*. Ideas are scored by a frozen statistical judge against sealed predictions, screened by a gatekeeper that only looks at their *form*, checked for novelty, killed by preregistered "death tests" run in a sandbox, and selected from one generation to the next. Every artefact is hash-sealed before it is read; every decisive number is traced to a result file.

**Why it exists.** Automated researchers are useful only if they resist the two ways an optimiser games a research loop: *grading itself* (the proposer, the judge and the experimenter share a mind) and *drifting into the familiar* (the loop rewards what the judge already likes). This repository is one concrete answer, built and audited over October 2026 by a single researcher with AI coding agents under written rules, and it is published so that others can read the guards, run the offline demonstrations and try to break them.

**Related work.** The principles this system is built on are already published, and none of them is claimed here; this repository adopts them and cites them. *Separating who proposes from who judges*: Qu, Chen, Wang ("Propose, Don't Judge: An Anytime-Valid Referee for LLM Agents That Mine Investment Factors", arXiv:2609.27051, 2026) separate the proposing agent from the referee that decides (pp. 1, 2, 4, 11) and claim the priority of that separation (pp. 2, 4); PROCTOR (Wahi, "LLM-as-a-Judge Is Not an Oracle", arXiv:2609.02246, 2026) also keeps the judge apart from what it judges (p. 4) and describes a silent fallback as a failure (pp. 1, 7). *A statistic computed outside the agents, by trusted code*: POPPER (Huang, Jin, Li, Li, Candès, Leskovec, "Automated hypothesis validation with agentic sequential falsifications", ICML 2025, PMLR 267:25372–25437; arXiv:2502.09858) already decides on free-form hypotheses produced by language models with an e-value rule and a 1/α threshold written outside the agents, so neither e-values nor that threshold are claimed here; a statistic computed outside the agents also appears in Qu, Chen, Wang (pp. 1, 10, 11), Sargsyan ("Structural Enforcement of Statistical Rigor in AI-Driven Discovery: A Functional Architecture", arXiv:2511.06701, 2026, pp. 8, 9, 11), CONFIRM (Wei et al., "CONFIRM: Governing Scientific Reporting for Trustworthy Agentic Neuroimaging", arXiv:2611.01449, 2026, p. 3), CADS (Chen et al., "CADS: Measuring the Incremental Contribution of LLM Proposers in Sequential Biological Screens", arXiv:2610.24597, 2026, p. 5), Wang et al. ("N×N E-valuation", arXiv:2608.06621, 2026, pp. 1, 3) and Zhang ("Hard to Fool: A Bounded LLM Research Loop for Quantitative Finance, and What Two Dozen Campaigns Found", arXiv:2611.06607, 2026, pp. 1, 3–4). *A plan sealed by hash before the data, and a hash-sealed record*: CONFIRM hashes its plan before any outcome is read (p. 3); Zhang binds the specification and the verdict code by SHA-256 before the data exist and keeps a hash-chained register with no fallback price source (pp. 3–4); Masked Erosion (So, Liu, Long, "Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents", arXiv:2610.38392, 2026) freezes and hashes the authors' predictions before each trial (pp. 4, 24); Qu, Chen, Wang state that order in principle (pp. 2, 10). Seeding the fresh draws by the hash of the commitment (section 2, step 7) is described here as an implementation choice. *Multiple comparisons, including the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure for e-values*: Qu, Chen, Wang (pp. 2, 4, 8); Sargsyan, with an online false discovery rate (pp. 1, 15–16, 23, 25); CADS (pp. 4–6); Wang et al., on 285 hypotheses written by models (pp. 25–26); CONFIRM (pp. 5–6, 14); Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut ("Valid Stopping in Adaptive Generator–Verifier Loops", arXiv:2610.06432, 2026, pp. 2–3). *The cheaters bench, the preregistered validation and the mutation bench (section 3.1)* are instruments of this system, and their sizes and numbers are reported as its measurements, not as ideas: an adaptive analyst who fools a reused holdout set, and an attack through the scores a leaderboard returns, have been prior art since 2015 (Dwork, Feldman, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Roth, "Generalization in Adaptive Data Analysis and Holdout Reuse", arXiv:1506.02629; Blum and Hardt, "The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions", arXiv:1502.04585); Qu, Chen, Wang already run scripted cheaters against an e-value referee (pp. 16, 29), validate their statistical tool under preregistration (pp. 8, 10, 14, 29, 36) and test deliberately faulty judges (p. 13); Masked Erosion mutates an acceptance gate (pp. 2, 8); PACE (Shawn, "PACE: Anytime-Valid Acceptance Tests for Self-Evolving Agents", arXiv:2606.08106, 2026) proposes unit tests for acceptors (p. 6); Sargsyan uses adversarial generations (pp. 27–29) and CADS adversarial contamination channels (pp. 9, 23). *The Goodhart attack (section 4)*: CONFIRM already serves its judge's diagnostics to a language-model proposer on 150 controls designed to be negative, with no candidate admitted (pp. 6, 9, 22); Qu, Chen, Wang name the threat (pp. 6, 18, 25) and return the referee's diagnostics to the proposing model, without an instruction to deceive (p. 12); REUSE (Sun, Zeng, She, Wang, arXiv:2609.33180, 2026) bounds a theoretical one-bit adversarial proposer (p. 32); PACE studies a world with no gain, without an attack (pp. 6–7); Sargsyan gives an adversarial instruction to a language model that knows the harness, aimed at the data boundary rather than at the statistic (pp. 27–28); arXiv:2611.00643 optimises a harness against a language-model judge (pp. 1, 2, 10), a precedent for the premise of the judge stages that use language models; Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut study a generator that adapts to its verifier (pp. 1–2). What section 4 measures is only this narrower form: on worlds where every promotion is false, a language-model proposer instructed to get a false card promoted by the statistical judge, given the judge's exact description as text and free in its proposals, with one arm that receives the judge's verdicts and one that does not, compared. *The judge's null*: the statistic is computed against a null formed by the known laws (the archive), so that a card is promoted only if its sealed prediction beats what the archive already predicts ("nothing beyond the archive"). As a loop that generates, ranks and evolves ideas, the system has a neighbour in Co-Scientist (Gottweis et al., Nature 655, 487–496, 2026; preprint "Towards an AI co-scientist", arXiv:2502.18864).

**Status (13 November 2026).** Pre-pilot stage. One full ideation round has been run with real agents (29 idea cards, 17 distinct ideas). The statistical judge has been exercised on a real bank of model episodes, on planted worlds, against 11 scripted cheaters (decisive validation, section 3.1) and against a language-model proposer that reads its exact description and sees its verdicts (Goodhart attack, clean rerun of 5 November, section 4). That attack found the judge's one declared gap; a new null (v9) that closes it has a written, counter-read proof, and its decisive validation, sealed on 3 November, returned "power lost", the outcome it expected in writing: v8 remains the production null until the project lead decides on that price. Attacked in turn as a candidate (verdict of 10 November; [status page](resultats/attaque-goodhart-v2/)), v9 "resists (at this size)" on the world inside its null, and at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, the outcome is "frontier crossed, robust": the proposer finds a law that replays above α where the archive alone does not pass (10 of the 17 laws promoted under v9 replay above α after Holm). 8 of 11 predictions held; reservations and open decisions on the status page. The death-test stage and the generation-to-generation loop have now run for real once, at the smallest size: after two runs of 12 November 2026 that stopped at the first proposer call ("not judgeable (stop)", code v66, sealed text v1), the third attempt under sealed text v2 (code v67, configuration v8) played both generations, and a fresh reader confirmed outcome C1, "the chain holds (covered class, this world, this size)": class covered by the v8 null, toy world S-V1, one and a half generations; nothing about the uncovered class, real data or the quality of the ideas; seven reservations, none changing the outcome; always reported with the out-of-class breach of the Goodhart attack ([status page](resultats/course-v67/)). Nothing here is a validated scientific result about AI control; it is an instrument, with its test suite, its mutation bench and its known gaps stated below.

